  "tour": {
    "title": "Genesis 6:1-4 and the Flood",
    "intro": "This tour reads Genesis 6:1-4 in context, compares the main interpretations of the sons of God, and explains how the Nephilim and the passage's placement prepare us for the flood.",
    "steps": [
      {
        "recordId": "listeners-commentary:podcast:417c1f86-com-2f63c731-32e0-3416-a86c-8ce581c0de7f",
        "startSec": 1126,
        "endSec": 1420,
        "why": "Begin with a direct walkthrough of the passage. The teacher presents three views—spiritual beings, human rulers, and the godly line—then says the key point is the passage's portrayal of “the decay and depravity of the human race.” He also argues that Genesis does not clearly say the Nephilim were the offspring of the unions.",
        "episodeTitle": "Genesis 4:1-6:8",
        "sourceId": "listeners-commentary",
        "source": "The Listener's Commentary",
        "audioUrl": "https://mcdn.podbean.com/mf/web/ba4rg9y6b4ksgv6v/genesis_41-6889j29.mp3",
        "durationSec": 1767,
        "adInsertionDrift": false
      },
      {
        "recordId": "forty-minutes-ot:podcast:a3cf54d8ac6db89ab2eedd131d56e107",
        "startSec": 1240,
        "endSec": 1600,
        "why": "This gives you a serious alternative to the angelic reading. The teacher says, “I don't buy into” the interpretation of rebellious angels and instead reads the sons of God as believers who marry those opposed to God. He also takes the 120 years as the interval before the flood and “a time for repentance.”",
        "episodeTitle": "40 Minutes In The Old Testament: Episode 11",
        "sourceId": "forty-minutes-ot",
        "source": "40 Minutes in the Old Testament",
        "audioUrl": "https://adbarker.com/stream/tfVE3XBxC9JCmb97VsZt7vpT/traffic.libsyn.com/secure/40minot/40_MIN_EP_11_Final.mp3?dest-id=245197",
        "durationSec": 2412,
        "adInsertionDrift": false
      },
      {
        "recordId": "bibleproject:podcast:genesis-1-11-q-r",
        "startSec": 2070,
        "endSec": 2350,
        "why": "Now hear the heavenly-beings reading and its limits. The speakers connect “sons of God” with the divine beings in Job, but emphasize that “the story doesn't say the Nephilim are their offspring.” They explain that the familiar angelic-union-and-giants version is expanded in the later Book of Enoch, which was influential but is not part of the biblical canon.",
        "episodeTitle": "Genesis 1-11 Question & Response",
        "sourceId": "bibleproject",
        "source": "BibleProject",
        "audioUrl": "https://afp-597195-injected.calisto.simplecastaudio.com/695767b0-cd40-4e6c-ac8c-ac6bc0df77ee/episodes/e1d03584-54ec-43f1-a199-59f940b9f1b5/audio/128/default.mp3?awCollectionId=695767b0-cd40-4e6c-ac8c-ac6bc0df77ee&awEpisodeId=e1d03584-54ec-43f1-a199-59f940b9f1b5",
        "durationSec": 2761,
        "adInsertionDrift": false
      },
      {
        "recordId": "bibleproject:podcast:gods-spirit-flood-narrative",
        "startSec": 840,
        "endSec": 1200,
        "why": "This clip explains the passage's literary role. The speakers read it as a negative mirror of Genesis 3: the sons of Elohim “see” and “take,” crossing boundaries and bringing corruption into the human world. They describe the Nephilim as “mighty warriors” and show how the story overturns ancient claims that hybrid divine-human rulers founded kingdoms with divine approval.",
        "episodeTitle": "God’s Spirit in the Flood Narrative",
        "sourceId": "bibleproject",
        "source": "BibleProject",
        "audioUrl": "https://afp-597195-injected.calisto.simplecastaudio.com/695767b0-cd40-4e6c-ac8c-ac6bc0df77ee/episodes/6fed2ba8-01ae-4fdb-8afe-9c7ab3232f32/audio/128/default.mp3?awCollectionId=695767b0-cd40-4e6c-ac8c-ac6bc0df77ee&awEpisodeId=6fed2ba8-01ae-4fdb-8afe-9c7ab3232f32",
        "durationSec": 3585,
        "adInsertionDrift": false
      },
      {
        "recordId": "spoken-gospel:podcast:edf9e365-6859-4f4d-947e-9cfc491ea29b",
        "startSec": 423,
        "endSec": 840,
        "why": "End with the clearest answer to why this appears immediately before the flood. The teacher says the passage shows that “the rebellion and the brokenness and the sin is getting worse”: instead of spreading God's image, humanity is spreading “a broken image.” The clip then connects the 120 years to the period before the flood and frames the scene as corruption spiraling out of control.",
        "episodeTitle": "Genesis 6-11: The Flood",
        "sourceId": "spoken-gospel",
        "source": "Spoken Gospel",
        "audioUrl": "https://traffic.megaphone.fm/SPG5000636430.mp3?updated=1664486134",
        "durationSec": 3274,
        "adInsertionDrift": true
      }
    ],
    "closing": "You will finish with both the textual caution and the larger narrative point: interpreters disagree about the sons of God, Genesis is restrained about the Nephilim, and the passage functions as a dark escalation of boundary-breaking violence before God's flood judgment.",
    "totalSeconds": 1711
  },
